    "evidence_text": "Headache, migraine, and structural brain lesions and function: population based Epidemiology of Vascular Ageing-MRI study\n\nOBJECTIVE To evaluate the association of overall and specific headaches with volume of white matter hyperintensities, brain infarcts, and cognition.   \n\n\nDESIGN Population based, cross sectional study.   \n\n\nSETTING Epidemiology of Vascular Ageing study, Nantes, France.   \n\n\nPARTICIPANTS 780 participants (mean age 69, 58.5% women) with detailed headache assessment.   \n\n\nCognitive function was assessed by a battery of tests including the mini-mental state examination.   \n\n\nAn association was found between any history of severe headache and increasing volume of white matter hyperintensities.\n\nThe association pattern was similar for all headache types.\n\nThe location of infarcts was predominantly outside the cerebellum and brain stem.\n\nEvidence was lacking for cognitive impairment for any headache type with or without brain lesions.   \n\n\nMigraine with aura was the only headache type associated with brain infarcts.\n\nEvidence that headache of any type by itself or in combination with brain lesions was associated with cognitive impairment was lacking.\n\nOBJECTIVE Chronic physical comorbidity is common in dementia.\n\nHowever, there is an absence of evidence to support good practice guidelines for attention to these problems.\n\nRESULTS Incontinence, hearing impairment, mobility impairment and undernutrition were consistently linearly associated with the presence of dementia and its severity across regions.\n\nOnly hearing impairment made a notable independent contribution to cognitive impairment.   \n\n\nTraditional Chinese acupuncture in tension-type headache: a controlled study.\n\nFive measures were used to assess symptom severity and treatment response: intensity, duration and frequency of headache pain episodes, headache index and analgesic intake.\n\nBefore the start of the study, each patient was administered the MMPI.\n\nA diffusion tensor magnetic resonance imaging study of corpus callosum from adult patients with migraine complicated with depressive/anxious disorder.\n\nBACKGROUND Emotional disorders, especially depression and anxiety, are with relatively higher incidence in migraine population.\n\nHowever, the mechanism of migraine complicated with depressive/anxious disorder remains unclear.   \n\n\nRESULTS There were significant differences in FA values at all locations of the CC among the 3 groups.\n\nThe apparent diffusion coefficient values of the above regions had no significant differences among these groups (P > .05).\n\nChronic daily headache. A clinical and psychological profile before and after treatment.\n\nOne patient had no scale elevation.\n\nSTAI 1,2 scores were high in both groups.\n\nSeveral psychosomatic symptoms and some migraine features were present in almost all patients.\n\nImprovement of headaches and a decrease of several MMPI and STAI 2 scores were observed.\n\nHowever, 12 of 20 patients showed a conversion V configuration after treatment.\n\nWith time, these patients may develop a depressive disorder.\n\nNeither treatment, however, eliminated headache problems.\n\nDocumented head injury in early adulthood and risk of Alzheimer's disease and other dementias.\n\nBACKGROUND The association between antecedent head injury and AD is inconsistent.   \n\n\nMETHODS The study had a population-based prospective historical cohort design.\n\nThe entire sample was then evaluated for dementia and AD using a multistage procedure.\n\nThere were 548 veterans with head injury and 1228 without head injury who completed all assigned stages of the study.\n\nThe authors estimated risk of dementia, specifically AD, using proportional hazards models.   \n\n\nResults were similar for dementia in general.\n\nThe results for mild head injury were inconclusive.\n\nHowever, the authors cannot exclude the possibility that other unmeasured factors may be influencing this association.\n\nA diffusion tensor magnetic resonance imaging study of brain tissue from patients with migraine.\n\nWhen present, average MD and FA values of T2 visible lesions were also measured.   \n\n\nNo differences were found in FA histogram derived metrics between migraine patients and healthy subjects.\n\nPsychiatric comorbidity in chronic daily headache.\n\nClinical evidence suggests that chronic daily headache (CDH) occurs in association with psychopathologies: previous studies have focused particularly on migraine.\n\n8.7 years).\n\nThe comorbidity of psychiatric disorders and headache has important implications as far as treatment is concerned.\n\nComorbidity of depressive and anxiety disorders in chronic daily headache and its subtypes.\n\nOBJECTIVE To investigate the frequency of depressive and anxiety disorders in patients with chronic daily headache.   \n\n\nRESULTS Two hundred sixty-one patients with chronic daily headache were recruited.\n\nThe mean age was 46 years, and 80% were women.\n\nBoth depressive and anxiety disorders were significantly more frequent in women.   \n\n\nThese results demonstrate that women and patients with transformed migraine are at higher risk of psychiatric comorbidity.",
